In this section, we show that if $G$ consists of two cycles sharing a
\section{Characterizing the $\ab{4}{2}$-Choosable Graphs: A Conjecture}
\label{sec:all42}
Having determined which 3-choosable-critical graphs are
$\ab{4}{2}$-choosable, the next natural step in investigating
$\ab{4}{2}$-choosability is to characterize \emph{all}
$\ab{4}{2}$-choosable graphs, mirroring Rubin's characterization of
the $2$-choosable graphs~\cite{rubin}. As Theorem~\ref{thm:main}
shows, the $\ab{4}{2}$-choosable graphs have considerably more variety
than the $2$-choosable graphs, so the proof of any such
characterization is likely to be much more involved than Rubin's
proof.

 Rubin observed that $G$ is
$2$-choosable if and only if its core is $2$-choosable, and the same
observation holds for $\ab{4}{2}$-choosability. It clearly
also suffices to consider only connected graphs, so we restrict to
the case where $G$ is connected with minimum degree at least $2$.
