HatChooser: A Python Program for (4,2)-Choosability
===================================================

Source code for the paper "On (4,2)-Choosable Graphs" by Jixian Meng,
Gregory J. Puleo, and Xuding Zhu.  (Journal of Graph Theory 85 (2),
412-428)

This program accepts a graph given in a symbolic format (described in
FORMAT.txt) and determines whether or not the graph is
(4,2)-choosable. In case the graph is (4,2)-choosable, the program
also allows the user to specify a "focal vertex", and returns all
"minimal solution sets" that can be forced at that vertex by list
assignments: that is, the program will output all inclusion-minimal
sets $S$ (up to relabeling of colors) such that there is a list
assignment $L$ where all proper $L$-colorings of the graph force $v$
to take a color-set from the set $S$.

Dependencies:
=============

This program requires the "cytonaut" package, which can be found at: https://bitbucket.org/gpuleo/cytonaut

Cytonaut, in turn, requires a copy of "nauty", as well as the
"networkx" package, in order to compile. See the build instructions
for Cytonaut for more information.

Usage:
=======

On a *nix system, the program can be invoked from the command line by running

./main.py filename

where "filename" is the name of a file containing the input graph, in
the format specified by FORMAT.txt.

The program will generate all possible "signatures" up to isomorphism
(i.e., all list assignments to the decision vertices).  For each
signature, the program generates all possible tuples $(A, \hat{X}_1,
\hat{X}_n)$ for the internal paths (as described in the paper) and,
for each tuple assignment, checks whether there is a precoloring of
the decision vertices that will allow extension of the coloring to the
internal paths for the given tuple assignment.

If a bad assignment is found, the program will output the signature as
well as the offending tuples (as can be seen by trying the file
"thetafail.ch").  Otherwise, if the graph is (4,2)-choosable, the
program will output all of the possible "minimal solution sets" for
the given focal vertex $v$: that is, all the inclusion-minimal sets
$S$, up to relabeling colors, such that there is a list assignment $L$
forcing $v$ to take a color-set from $S$ in every $L$-coloring.

License
=======

This source code has been released into the public domain via a CC0
license. See COPYING.txt for legalese.

If you find this source code useful in your research, please consider
citing the original paper:

"On (4,2)-Choosable Graphs" by Jixian Meng, Gregory J. Puleo, and
Xuding Zhu. Journal of Graph Theory 85 (2), 412-428

