-{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 32, "mixer_steps": 288, "chord_count": 7}, "seed": 2142020714, "solved": "failed", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": false, "verify_reason": "affine parity rule does not alternate on every cycle edge", "reply": "To find the exact symbolic (L,2)-colouring, we need to find an affine parity formula `b(x) = (popcount(mask AND x) mod 2) XOR offset` such that adjacent vertices evaluate to different bits. This means for every edge `(u, v)`, we must have `popcount(mask AND (u XOR v)) \u2261 1 (mod 2)`.\n\nThe vertices in the cycle are generated using a 32-bit Gray code `g(i) = i XOR (i >> 1)`. For cycle edges, the Gray code changes by exactly one bit. Hence, if we apply the inverse of the circuit transformations, the target parity of `popcount(mask AND x)` corresponds exactly to the parity of the original Gray code `g(i)`, which perfectly alternates along the cycle. Since all chords join odd and even positions in the cycle, the exact same condition seamlessly validates the chords.\n\nWe trace the effect of the mixer circuit forwards, tracking how it linearly maps the mask:\nStarting with the initial mask of 32 ones `mask = 0xFFFFFFFF`, we apply the same sequence of XOR (`C c t` meaning `mask[c] ^= mask[t]`) and SWAP (`S a b` meaning `swap(mask[a], mask[b])`) operations:\n- For `C c t`, the new value of bit `c` in the mask is the XOR of its old value and the value of bit `t`.\n- For `S a b`, the positions simply exchange their mask bits.\n\nBy applying these exact 288 operations in order onto the 32-bit initial mask `[1]*32`, the mask bits flip and swap until we reach the final state. \nThe remaining `1`s in the final mask are precisely at the bit positions: 0, 3, 4, 8, 9, 12, 20, 21, 22, 24, 25, and 29.\n\nSumming the powers of 2 for these set bits:\n2^0 + 2^3 + 2^4 + 2^8 + 2^9 + 2^12 + 2^20 + 2^21 + 2^22 + 2^24 + 2^25 + 2^29 \n= 1 + 8 + 16 + 256 + 512 + 4096 + 1048576 + 2097152 